SHACL: pyshacl (Python) or rdf-validate-shacl (JS).

PROV-O: rdflib (Python) or n3 (JS).

Recommended for web:

Frontend: React or Svelte.

Backend: FastAPI (Python) or Express (Node.js).

Schema validation: jsonschema or ajv.

SHACL: pyshacl or rdf-validate-shacl.

Storage: SQLite (local) or PostgreSQL (server).

Shared:

Rule engine: plain functions, no framework dependency.

Label and anchoring engines: pure functions, unit-testable.

JSON Schema: Draft 2020-12.

SHACL: SHACL Core.

UI/UX Specification
Layout: Left sidebar with panel navigation (13 panels, in order). Main area with active panel form. Right sidebar with live preview of derived label, anchoring status, and flags. Bottom bar with validation status and save/export buttons.

Navigation: Linear progression enforced. Can go back. Cannot skip forward unless panel is optional. Panel completion indicator per panel. Save disabled until all required panels pass validation.

Error display: Inline field errors (red border, message below field). Rule violations shown in a collapsible panel with rule number and offending field. Derived label and anchoring status always visible in right sidebar, never hidden.

Flags display: not_determinable_flag shown as a banner above the label. provisional_flag shown as a badge next to the label. threshold_sensitive_flag shown as a badge. regime_not_determinable shown as a badge. identity_partition_sensitive shown as a badge.

Validation Engine Design
The rule engine executes rules in numeric order (1 to 33). Each rule is a function:

function ruleN(record) returns ValidationResult.

If not trigger(record): return PASS.

If condition_satisfied(record): return PASS.

Return FAIL with rule number N, offending field, and message.

Rules run at two points: on panel exit (subset relevant to that panel) and on save (all rules).

Testing Strategy
Unit tests:

Each rule function tested with a passing record and a failing record.

Label derivation tested against all seven labels and all unclassified reasons.

Anchoring derivation tested against all six values.

Entailment basis tested against E1, E2, E3, E4, not_determinable.

Scale-separation calculator tested against the worked example in Artifact 1, Section 11.2 (ratio 18.2, expansion parameter approximately 0.055).

Integration tests:

Full record built panel by panel, saved, reloaded, re-validated.

PROV-O export tested against the Turtle serialization in Artifact 1, Section 8.5.

SHACL validation tested against the worked examples.

Worked-example tests:

Artifact 1, Section 11.1 (Fröhlich/Eliashberg): label STAG-Definitional, anchoring not_applicable, not_determinable flag attached.

Artifact 1, Section 11.2 (two-layer Ising): label STAG-Discovered, anchoring preliminary, not_determinable flag attached.

Artifact 1, Section 11.3 (two-band superconductor): label STAG-None, anchoring not_assessed.

Artifact 1, Section 11.4 (glass transition): label STAG-Unclassified, reason inadmissible_partition.

Artifact 1, Section 11.5 (QCD crossover): label STAG-Unclassified, reason not_tested.

Regression tests:

Supersession chain acyclicity.

Empty-W convention consistency.

Tie-breaking rule consistency.

Build Phases
Phase 1. Data model and schema. Implement JSON Schema, internal state object, persistence layer.

Phase 2. Rule engine. Implement all 32 rules as pure functions with unit tests.

Phase 3. Label derivation engine. Implement Section 7.6 decision tree with unit tests.

Phase 4. Anchoring engine. Implement Section 1.2 conditions with unit tests.

Phase 5. Entailment basis engine. Implement Section 7.3 decision tree with unit tests.

Phase 6. Scale-separation calculator. Implement computation and anchoring flag.

Phase 7. Partition audit engine. Implement Sections 5.8, 7.7, 7.8.

Phase 8. UI panels. Implement 13 panels with navigation and error display.

Phase 9. Live preview. Implement right sidebar with label, anchoring status, flags.

Phase 10. Save and export. Implement JSON save, PROV-O export, SHACL validation report.

Phase 11. Worked-example tests. Load the five worked examples and verify outputs.

Phase 12. Integration testing and hardening.

Dependencies
Python path: jsonschema, pyshacl, rdflib, PyQt6 (or Tauri if using Rust plus web frontend), pytest.

JavaScript path: ajv, rdf-validate-shacl, n3, React or Svelte, Express or Fastify, Vitest or Jest.

Shared: JSON Schema Draft 2020-12, SHACL Core, PROV-O vocabulary.

Deployment Notes
Desktop: package as standalone executable (PyInstaller for Python, Tauri build for Rust plus web). No server required. Local file storage.

Web: deploy backend with FastAPI or Express. Frontend as static bundle. Storage in SQLite (single-user) or PostgreSQL (multi-user). HTTPS required for any multi-user deployment.

Reproducibility: record software versions, schema versions, and rule engine version in every saved record under protocol_cross_reference.

Open Questions to Resolve Before Building
Question 1. Which runtime? Python or TypeScript. Decision affects UI framework, schema library, and packaging.

Question 2. Desktop or web first? Desktop is simpler for single-annotator use. Web is needed for collaboration and journal submission checking.

Question 3. How is the annotator's independence rating captured? The documents specify the rating procedure (list assumptions, classify as independent, shared_assumption, or not_assessed) but not the UI. Decision needed: free-text lists plus a dropdown, or a structured assumption-entry widget.

Question 4. How is the entailment basis decision tree presented? The documents specify a five-step procedure. Decision needed: linear wizard, or a single form with conditional fields.

Question 5. How are alternative partitions loaded for the partition sensitivity check? The documents require at least one alternative admissible partition. Decision needed: manual entry, or a partition registry lookup.

Question 6. How is the SHACL validation report displayed? Decision needed: inline in the save panel, or as a separate export.

Question 7. What is the minimum viable set of panels for a first release? The documents specify 13 panels. Decision needed: build all 13 at once, or build core-tier panels first (1 to 9) and add extension-tier panels (10 to 12) later.

Question 8. How is the audit log structured? The documents require recording rules checked, fields populated, and flags attached, but not the format. Decision needed: JSON lines, or a structured object.

Question 9. Where does the PROV-O export go? Decision needed: same file as the JSON record, separate file, or both.

Question 10. How does the editor handle the not_determinable propagation? The documents specify that not_determinable propagates from the entailment basis to intervention_classification to cross_domain_counterfactual_dependence. Decision needed: automatic propagation, or manual confirmation at each step.

